# Decision record: closed 2026-08-25 on the merge-authority fork.
# Floor invariant 10's blanket human-merge clause was over-generalized from a
# narrower concern and blocked fleet migration; the face carries the collapse.
"@context": "urn:bedrock:context/v1"
"@id": "urn:bedrock:vertex/decision-agent-merge-authority"
"@type": "urn:bedrock:ontology/Decision"
label: "Agents merge green gates; humans merge forks in the road"
references:
  - "urn:bedrock:vertex/invariant-10-git-rules"
  - "urn:bedrock:vertex/invariant-11-ci-rules"
statement: >
  Chose to replace floor invariant 10's blanket "a human merges the default
  branch" with a two-case rule: an agent merges its own PR once the gate is
  green (agreed features, ordinary work, propagations), and a human merges only
  at a fork in the road — a choice between architectures, removal of a working
  feature or path, or a blast radius that cascades across repositories. The
  original clause was written to prevent unsupervised change, but it
  generalized a narrow concern into law and made fleet-wide migration
  impossible: a bedrock change could never land in every repository without a
  human merging N pull requests by hand. Rejected: blanket human merge (blocks
  migration, and the gate already supplies the mechanical check the clause was
  really asking for); blanket agent merge (removes the human checkpoint exactly
  where judgment about physics is required); a per-repository allowlist of
  merge-authorized agents (configuration drift, and liveness of the list would
  need its own oracle). Revisit if agents merge across a fork in the road in
  practice, which would mean the two cases are not discriminable from a diff.
timestamp: "2026-08-25T00:00:00"
body: |
  ## Why the old clause was wrong, not just inconvenient

  It was authored to stop changes landing without oversight. That intent is
  correct and survives — but the clause encoded the intent as "a human
  performs every merge," which is a mechanism, not the intent. Once a required
  gate exists, the mechanical part of oversight is the gate's job: no PR merges
  onto red, agent or human. What is left for the human is the part a gate
  cannot judge.

  ## The discriminator

  The line is not size, and it is not risk in the abstract. It is whether the
  decision is a fork in the road — a choice between paths where taking one
  forecloses the other, and where the cost of being wrong is paid somewhere the
  diff does not show. Deleting a working feature, changing an architecture after
  discovering the original cannot work, or altering something that cascades into
  every downstream repository: those are the human's. Raising a byte cap,
  implementing a feature already agreed, propagating a stub: those are not.

  An agent cannot reliably see the space such a decision sits in, because the
  relevant context is often not in the repository at all. That is the whole
  reason the case is reserved, and it is why the reservation is stated in terms
  of the decision's shape rather than the change's size.

  ## Why this unblocks migration

  A bedrock change is a schema migration across every adopting repository. A
  database is not migrated halfway. Propagation opens a PR per repository
  deterministically; an agent then walks them, waits for each gate, resolves the
  conflicts that arise, merges, and reports the outliers back into the
  originating PR. Under the old clause that final step required a human in N
  repositories, so in practice the fleet would drift and nobody would notice —
  which is precisely the failure the graph exists to prevent.

  ## What still holds

  Force push does not exist. Nothing pushed is deleted. One writer per ref.
  PRs always. No merge onto a red gate. A human may also grant merge authority
  explicitly for a change that would otherwise be reserved.
